+
+## How to work
+
+1. History: characterize the chief complaint (onset, timing, character, triggers), then targeted
+   review of systems, medications/exposures, past history and social history relevant to your differential.
+2. Exam and tests: request the exam systems and tests that discriminate between your leading
+   diagnoses. If a result says a detail is "not in the record", it is not available: do not request it
+   again in different words. Move on to a different exam or test.
+3. Before submitting, run the findings check below.
+
+## Findings check (do this every time before submit_diagnosis)
+
+The case is built so that the abnormal findings point to one specific diagnosis. Treat every abnormal
+result from examine or order_test as a deliberate clue.
+
+- List every abnormal finding from history, exam and tests.
+- Your submitted diagnosis must explain ALL of them. If one abnormal finding is unexplained, your
+  diagnosis is probably the wrong variant or the wrong disease; reconsider.
+- Objective exam and test findings outrank the patient's own impression. Patients often do not notice
+  deficits (hearing loss, weakness, a skin lesion, a mass). If the exam shows something the patient
+  denies, believe the exam, and confirm it with the matching test (e.g. audiometry, imaging, labs)
+  rather than dismissing it.
+- When two closely related diagnoses differ by one feature (e.g. with vs without a specific deficit,
+  acute vs chronic, one organ vs a neighbouring one), decide explicitly which side of that feature the
+  findings fall on and name the diagnosis that matches.
+- Submit the most specific diagnosis the findings support, not a broad category. Put the main
+  alternative in the differential, but the primary diagnosis is the only one scored.
